 * Design Title: System Optimization: Elimination of Friction & Resistance (Project Zero-Drag)
 * Lead Independent Designer: C. Matthew Blackwell
 * Date: 2024.10.27
 * Scale & Classification: 1:10 at A1 | Code: -044-F7G8-B
Core Architectural Subsystems
 * Active Magnetic Levitation Array: Implements dynamic electromagnetic fields to achieve complete rotor suspension, eliminating mechanical surface-to-surface contact under operational loads.
 * Redundant Fluid Film Bearing: Provides secondary fail-safe damping using a precision-controlled micro-channel fluid interface to stabilize high-frequency shaft precession.
 * Diamond-Like Carbon (DLC) Nano-Coating: Applied to all critical micro-interfacing boundaries to suppress adhesive wear, lower boundary friction coefficients, and ensure chemical inertness.
 * Minimal-Resistance Seal Geometry: Utilizes labyrinth-pathway profiles and non-contact gas/fluid barrier dynamics to isolate high-pressure cavities without inducing parasitic drag torque.
Engineering Specifications & Materials
 * Rotor Assembly: Aerospace-grade titanium-aluminum alloy (Ti-6Al-4V) core wrapped in high-permeability grain-oriented silicon steel laminations for optimized magnetic flux distribution.
 * Stator Housings: High-purity nickel-iron soft magnetic alloys engineered for minimal hysteresis loss and thermal dimensional stability under continuous high-torque cycles.
 * Surface Treatments: PVD (Physical Vapor Deposition) deposited DLC thin films maintaining a thickness tolerance of 1.5\,\mu\text{m} to 2.0\,\mu\text{m} with a surface hardness exceeding 30\,\text{GPa}.
 * Tolerances: Precision machining class IT5 to IT6, ensuring radial air-gap consistency within \pm 12\,\mu\text{m} across active magnetic poles.
Implementation Workflow & Assembly Guidelines
 * Phase I - Substrate Preparation: Clean core mechanical components in an ultrasonic iso-propyl alcohol bath prior to vacuum chamber plasma etching and DLC coating deposition.
 * Phase II - Magnetic Core Calibration: Calibrate Hall-effect position sensors and PID controllers for the active magnetic levitation array to guarantee sub-millisecond dynamic centering response.
 * Phase III - Fluid Integration: Flush micro-channels with filtered, synthetic low-viscosity perfluoropolyether lubricants to establish the redundant fluid film boundary layer.
 * Phase IV - Final Validation: Execute high-speed spin-down testing under vacuum to quantify parasitic windage and verify zero mechanical contact telemetry.
